I chose **candidate 4**. This is a rough estimate worked out by hand, not an exact simulation of each plan.

1. **Daily plans (0 and 3) lose on order fees.** Ordering every day costs 40 × 28 = 1120. Every-other-day plans pay about 14 × 40 = 560. Candidate 0 also orders about 1.05 of the forecast for each single day. A single day's demand varies by about 30%, so it will often run short (9 margin plus 6 penalty lost on each missing unit of product 1). Candidate 3 has a buffer of about 1.19, but it still pays the extra 560 in fees.

2. **Candidate 2 (every 3 days) risks hitting the 224 storage limit.** Around days 15 and 18, morning stock comes to about 215–225 units: product 1 left over, plus fresh product 0 (105 and 64), plus new product 1. Anything over the limit is lost, and with random demand it will sometimes go over. It also holds product 1 for 3 of its 4 days of shelf life, so little can carry over to the next delivery. It saves only about 120 in fees compared with the every-other-day plans.

3. **The every-other-day plans differ only in the size of the buffer.** For product 1, measured against two days of forecast demand:
   - candidate 5: about 0.90 — too many shortages;
   - candidate 7: about 1.00 — below the average demand multiplier of 1.046, so shortages are still frequent;
   - candidate 6: about 1.055;
   - candidate 4: about 1.10;
   - candidate 1: about 1.25.

   On a two-day window demand varies by about 22%. A unit short costs about 15 and an expired unit costs 10, so the best buffer is roughly 1.10–1.15.

   Candidate 1 orders more than that every cycle and never adjusts. Leftover product 1 builds up and expires. Around day 17 it also goes over the limit: 168 of product 1, plus 46 and about 18 of product 0, plus leftovers comes to about 245. Product 0 is accepted first, so product 1 is the part that gets lost. Candidate 4 at about 1.10 stays near 213, just under the limit. It covers demand well and wastes little.
